#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:6
msgid "Fragment Specifiers"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:8
msgid ""
"**Fragment specifiers** tell the macro compiler what category of syntax to "
"expect and capture as a meta-variable. Here are the most commonly used "
"specifiers:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:12
msgid ""
"`ident`: An identifier (i.e., variable or item names such as `foo` or `_x`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:13
msgid ""
"`expr`: A single expression (e.g., `2 + 2`, `a.method()`, `match x { ... }`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:14
msgid ""
"`literal`: A literal value (e.g., `\"hello\"`, `42`, `true`, or `-4.0_f32`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:15
msgid "`stmt`: A single statement without a semicolon (e.g., `let x = 5`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:16
msgid "`pat`: A pattern (e.g., `[_, 0, ..]`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:17
msgid "`ty`: A type (e.g., `i32`, `Vec<String>`, `&str`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:18
msgid ""
"`path`: A path of segments separated by `::` (e.g., "
"`std::collections::HashMap`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:20
msgid ""
"`item`: A complete item definition (e.g., `fn foo() {}`, `struct Bar;`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:21
msgid ""
"`tt`: A single token tree (recall that this is either a single token or a "
"bracketed token group)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:24
msgid "Less common specifiers include:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:26
msgid "`lifetime`: A lifetime like `'a`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:27
msgid ""
"`block`: A block of statements enclosed in braces (e.g., `{ let x = 5; x }`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:28
msgid ""
"`vis`: An optional visibility specifier in an item, e.g. `pub` or "
"`pub(crate)`, or nothing."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:30
msgid ""
"`meta`: The possible _contents_ of a `#[...]` attribute (not the full "
"attribute with hash and brackets)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:35
msgid ""
"Explain that fragment specifiers act like type annotations for macro "
"parameters. They restrict what tokens can match."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:37
msgid ""
"For example, if you use `$x:expr`, the input must be parsable as a valid "
"Rust expression."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:39
msgid ""
"When the macro is expanded, the compiler inserts the matched AST fragment "
"directly, which guarantees semantic structure and operator precedence."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:41
msgid ""
"There is overlap between a number of these specifiers, e.g. we could write "
"our own pattern for blocks using `stmt` instead of using `block` directly. "
"In general, try to reach for the most semantically appropriate fragment "
"specifier where possible, which will catch more syntactic edge cases and "
"integrate more seamlessly into the language."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers.md:46
msgid ""
"There are some additional, obscure fragment specifiers such as `expr_2021` "
"and `pat_param`."
msgstr ""
